 "summaryEn": "<p>This is a short poem about a <b>kitchen</b>. It starts in a rich valley where rice, wheat, sugarcane, grapes and oranges grow, and goats, hens, ducks and pigs feed together.</p><p>The poet asks: who left this kitchen here? Maybe a great-great-great-grandmother, from so long ago that nobody even knows if she lived.</p><p>Then the poem shows the kitchen's never-ending work: chopping, cleaning fish, grinding, tasting, washing, wiping. <b>Someone is always working here.</b> And at the very end — someone is always crying with hunger. Big idea: kitchen work never stops, and it was mostly done by mothers and grandmothers for hundreds of years.</p>",
